From 0274f0e9da3e0a82fe70848ed92eba48f5f0f378 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Til Goldan <til@goldan.dev>
Date: Wed, 8 Jul 2026 15:50:52 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] thumb: don't jail a lone touch that's actively holding the
 click button

tp_thumb_ignored_for_gesture() excludes a touch from cursor motion
whenever thumb detection has classified it as THUMB_STATE_JAILED (among
other states). tp_thumb_update_touch() jails any single touch (no other
fingers down) that lands below thumb.upper_thumb_line, and on hardware
without pressure/size sensing this happens unconditionally, regardless
of the click method configured (LIBINPUT_CONFIG_CLICK_METHOD_CLICKFINGER
included -- thumb detection doesn't look at the click method at all).

That makes an ordinary 1-finger click-and-drag starting near the bottom
of the pad dead on arrival: the touch is jailed the instant it lands,
and stays jailed until it either escapes the button-area's 5mm movement
threshold (a separate, click-method-specific mechanism) or trips the
thumb code's own speed-based escape (t->speed.exceeded_count >= 10),
which requires moving fast repeatedly, not just far. A slow or short
drag never triggers either escape and the cursor simply doesn't move.

A touch that is the *only* one down and is actively holding the
clickpad button pressed cannot be a passively resting thumb -- it's the
touch causing the click. Bypass jailing for exactly that case. This is
deliberately restricted to a single touch: with two or more fingers
down, a thumb resting on the button while another finger drags is
exactly the scenario the jailing logic is meant to protect (e.g.
click-drag with a 3fg/4fg gesture, or holding the button with a thumb
while scrolling with two fingers), so that case is untouched.

Stacked on top of bazzite-til's 0001/0002/0003 patches.
---
 src/evdev-mt-touchpad-thumb.c | 10 ++++++++++
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/evdev-mt-touchpad-thumb.c b/src/evdev-mt-touchpad-thumb.c
index 876fe801..c622d790 100644
--- a/src/evdev-mt-touchpad-thumb.c
+++ b/src/evdev-mt-touchpad-thumb.c
@@ -148,6 +148,16 @@ tp_thumb_ignored_for_tap(const struct tp_dispatch *tp, const struct tp_touch *t)
 bool
 tp_thumb_ignored_for_gesture(const struct tp_dispatch *tp, const struct tp_touch *t)
 {
+	/* A lone touch that is actively holding the clickpad button down
+	 * can't be a passively resting thumb -- it's the touch causing the
+	 * click, so don't exclude it from cursor motion during a 1fg
+	 * click-and-drag. Only bypass jailing for a single touch: with two
+	 * or more fingers down, a thumb resting on the button while another
+	 * finger drags is exactly the scenario this jailing exists for.
+	 */
+	if (tp->buttons.is_clickpad && tp->buttons.state && tp->nfingers_down <= 1)
+		return false;
+
 	return (tp->thumb.detect_thumbs && tp->thumb.index == t->index &&
 		(tp->thumb.state == THUMB_STATE_JAILED ||
 		 tp->thumb.state == THUMB_STATE_SUPPRESSED ||
